2186045463 13194139533618 true] [17592186069287 1145 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139533618 true] [17592186069291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533618 true] [17592186069291 1145 "The graph breaks at $x=1,2$ and $4$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1,2,4.$" 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242123 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1089 "q-102115" 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/d1d91b2d791d69c512a6bd2e7d523563de076a7f3afda53513234e799152ef8d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1113 #uuid "d542cfea-06a6-5d32-836f-df32a05c8681" 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1138 false 13194139533618 true] [17592186242122 1145 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a break at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHen